<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 6 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">City-States and Leagues</h1><p class="path-tagline">How single cities and their alliances outfought and outtraded empires.</p><p class="path-intro">Not every great power was an empire. Some were single cities with harbours, fleets and ruthless bookkeeping. This path visits the city-states that punched far above their size - Athens and Carthage in the ancient Mediterranean, Venice and Novgorod at the two ends of medieval Europe's trade, and the Hanseatic league of cities that ran the northern seas without a king at all.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Navigator</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 900 BCE – 86 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Athens</span><span class="path-step-why">The democracy that built an Aegean empire on triremes and tribute.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/punic-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 814 BCE – 146 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Punic Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Carthage: a merchant city that fought three wars with Rome for the western Mediterranean.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/republic-of-venice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 697 CE – 1797 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Republic of Venice</span><span class="path-step-why">The lagoon republic that turned shipping lanes into an empire of islands and counting houses.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1204</span><span class="path-step-name">Fourth Crusade</span><span class="path-step-why">Venice at its most ruthless: a crusade redirected to its commercial rivals' capital.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/novgorod-republic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-transfiguration-on-ilyina-street.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1136 CE – 1478 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Novgorod Republic</span><span class="path-step-why">The merchant republic of the Russian north, where the assembly could dismiss a prince.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/hanseatic-league"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bryggen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1356 CE – 1669 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Hanseatic League</span><span class="path-step-why">A league of trading cities that made treaties, fought wars and set prices from London to Novgorod.</span></span></a></li></ol>
